    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "1b4a3633-dd2d-3c61-9d13-0ea8b5128582",
                "word": "exiguity",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "ff155298-34de-4b2c-ab54-c3752f261070",
                        "entry_id": "1b4a3633-dd2d-3c61-9d13-0ea8b5128582",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "exiguities"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                        "entry_id": "1b4a3633-dd2d-3c61-9d13-0ea8b5128582",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The quality of being meagre or scanty."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ba75d395-9338-4416-aa5f-09cc424d9f30",
                                "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                                "text": "The exiguity and ſmallneſſe of ſome ſeeds extending to large productions is one of the magnalities of nature, ſomewhat illuſtrating the work of the Creation, and vaſt production from nothing."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7ed109c6-d946-4596-a443-fdf94abd88a7",
                                "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                                "text": "Of the nature of Mr. Knott himself Watt remained in particular ignorance. Of the many excellent reasons for this, two seemed to Watt to merit mention: on the one hand the exiguity of the material propounded to his senses, and on the other the decay of these."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "faef5fd3-b244-4715-95fb-d1f5c74e2da6",
                                "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                                "text": "We have yet to treat the exiguity of the accounting framework and this exiguity draws away the interest to any empirical utilisation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "449ff376-1dfd-44c5-876c-1715ca5a2924",
                                "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                                "text": "However, despite its exiguity, the vicarage did maintain an independent existence as a benefice, and the College continued to make presentations to the bishop of Worcester."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ea46549b-38c8-4157-80f0-d4a5d8337df0",
                                "sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                                "text": "Some undertakings, however, required so much manpower that farmers had to recruit their neighbors. […] These collaborations integrated the neighborhood and established it as more than a mere locality where farmers happened to live. They were one means by which to rise above exiguities and weather the turbulences in a precarious world."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ea92091d-95af-4104-9e31-cbc277cb4fce",
                        "source_entry_id": "1b4a3633-dd2d-3c61-9d13-0ea8b5128582",
                        "source_sense_id": "33ce03eb-2541-338a-bab6-bfdc50d98f76",
                        "source_sense_description": "quality of being meagre or scanty — see also meagreness, scantiness",
                        "target_lang_word": "exiguïté",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "exiguïté",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9da4b4ab-e2ca-3f8d-a7a4-363c0874d18a",
                                        "entry_id": "b27edb1d-d8c5-3cd2-b66c-6dbab5d5b16a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Caractère de ce qui est exigu."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ef0ed7b5-a765-4e3e-b97d-438df0a7fdef",
                                                "sense_id": "9da4b4ab-e2ca-3f8d-a7a4-363c0874d18a",
                                                "text": "Avant la fin du dîner, Marsillat sortit pour donner l’avoine à sa jument Fanchon, qu’il avait installée dans une grange un peu isolée du village, à cause de l’exiguïté de l’écurie du presbytère."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "331f6862-c00b-40a7-81f0-491ef3b92b73",
                                                "sense_id": "9da4b4ab-e2ca-3f8d-a7a4-363c0874d18a",
                                                "text": "C’était le temps des exiguïtés : journaux, livres, publications de colportage, tout se réduisait au simple in-octavo pour les plus grandes mesures."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a7ce195d-8e1c-44e3-9df4-8e56a0238664",
                                                "sense_id": "9da4b4ab-e2ca-3f8d-a7a4-363c0874d18a",
                                                "text": "De ce double point de vue : l’habitabilité des mondes et l’exiguïté de la Terre, surgiront des conclusions qui élèveront à la certitude philosophique la probabilité de la Pluralité des Mondes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3990c9be-7198-4b68-b540-a89dd80cd328",
                                                "sense_id": "9da4b4ab-e2ca-3f8d-a7a4-363c0874d18a",
                                                "text": "Âgée de trente-trois ans, toujours vêtue de laine noire, timide, effacée, elle avait une tête trop petite, comprimée à droite et à gauche, et d’où le nez jaillissait comme une protestation contre une pareille exiguïté."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
